{
 "definitions": {
  "trivial": "the target is linear in one input: a straight-line fit to one input explains it (R^2 >= 0.999 on the fitting rows; formula problems on 256 points of the declared ranges)",
  "low-signal": "the inputs barely predict the target: a random forest and a linear fit (shuffled 5-fold CV) both reach R^2 < 0.05 on the fitting rows (data without an exact law)",
  "target-leak": "the target is an exact function of other inputs it is computed from (e.g. a total and its parts)",
  "tiny": "fewer than 20 fitting rows, or no more rows than inputs plus one (underdetermined even for a linear fit)",
  "ordered": "the rows are sorted by an input and the authors' split is random or by folds, so a test row usually sits between two fitting rows (interpolation)"
